In Hibernate/JPA (Spring Boot), caching has two levels: L1 (first-level) and L2 (second-level).
Interviewers usually mean these when they ask about L1 vs L2 cache — not Spring’s @Cacheable alone.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 Level 1 (L1) Cache — Session / Persistence Context Cache

What it is
- Enabled by default in Hibernate.
- Scoped to one Session / EntityManager (one persistence context / usually one transaction).
- Stores entities already loaded in that session so the same entity is not fetched again from DB within the same session.

Flow

Request / Transaction
        ↓
   EntityManager / Session
        ↓
   L1 Cache (in-memory, per session)
        ↓
   Database (only on first load)

Example

User u1 = entityManager.find(User.class, 1L);  // hits DB, stores in L1
User u2 = entityManager.find(User.class, 1L);  // L1 hit → NO second DB call
// u1 == u2  (same instance in that persistence context)

Key points
- Automatic — you don’t configure it.
- Private to one session — other sessions / app instances don’t see it.
- Cleared when the session/persistence context closes (typically end of transaction).
- Guarantees identity: same ID → same Java object inside one session.

Limitations
- Not shared across requests/sessions.
- Lost when transaction ends.
- Does not help if many concurrent users need the same data (each has its own L1).

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 Level 2 (L2) Cache — SessionFactory / Shared Cache

What it is
- Optional — must be enabled and configured.
- Scoped to SessionFactory / EntityManagerFactory (shared across sessions).
- Stores entity data so different sessions can reuse it without hitting DB again.

Flow

Session A                    Session B
    ↓                            ↓
 L1 (session A)              L1 (session B)
    ↓                            ↓
         └──→  L2 Cache  ←──────┘
                  ↓
               Database

Example providers
- Ehcache
- Caffeine (with Hibernate cache region factory)
- Infinispan
- Redis (via custom / 3rd-party region factory)
- Hazelcast

How it works (conceptually)

1. Session looks in its L1 → miss
2. Looks in L2 → hit → copy into L1 → return
3. If L2 miss → query DB → put in L1 and L2

Enable (Hibernate / Spring Boot style)

application.properties / yml:

spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.cache.use_second_level_cache=true
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.cache.use_query_cache=true
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.cache.region.factory_class=...

Entity must be cacheable:

@Entity
@Cacheable
@org.hibernate.annotations.Cache(usage = CacheConcurrencyStrategy.READ_WRITE)
public class User {
    ...
}

Concurrency strategies (important for interviews)
| Strategy            | Meaning                                      |
| ------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| READ_ONLY           | Data never changes (safest / fastest)        |
| NONSTRICT_READ_WRITE| Occasional inconsistency OK                  |
| READ_WRITE          | Soft locks; safer for updatable entities     |
| TRANSACTIONAL       | Fully transactional (needs JTA + provider)   |

What L2 stores
- Entity data (dehydrated state by ID), not the live managed entity instance.
- Can also cache collections and query results (query cache is separate but related).

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 L1 vs L2 (Quick Comparison)

| Point              | L1 Cache                         | L2 Cache                              |
| ------------------ | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Enabled by default | Yes                              | No                                   |
| Scope              | One Session / EntityManager      | SessionFactory (shared)              |
| Shared?            | No                               | Yes (across sessions in same JVM / cluster depending on provider) |
| Lifetime           | Until session closes             | Until eviction / TTL / invalidate    |
| Purpose            | Avoid repeat DB hits in one unit of work | Reuse data across many requests   |
| Config needed      | None                             | Yes (provider + @Cacheable entity)   |

What “Until eviction / TTL / invalidate” means (L2 lifetime)

L1 dies with the session. L2 can outlive many transactions — an entry leaves L2 only when:

1. Eviction
   Cache is full (or hits size/policy limit) → provider removes older / least-used entries.
   Example: max 10,000 users → when full, LRU drops the least recently used one.

2. TTL (Time To Live)
   Entry is kept only for a configured time, then expires automatically.
   Example: TTL = 10 minutes → after 10 minutes gone; next find() hits DB (and may refill L2).

3. Invalidate
   Something deliberately removes / marks the entry stale — usually after update/delete
   so other sessions don’t read outdated data.
   Example: user updated → that entity’s L2 entry is invalidated → next read reloads from DB.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 Lookup Order

When you call find(id):

1. Check L1 → HIT → return
2. Check L2 → HIT → put into L1 → return
3. Hit Database → put into L1 (+ L2 if enabled) → return

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 L2 vs Spring Cache (@Cacheable) — don’t mix them up

Hibernate L1 / L2
- Works at persistence / entity level inside JPA/Hibernate.
- Transparent when loading entities via EntityManager / repositories (for L1 always; L2 when enabled).

Spring Cache (@Cacheable, Redis, Caffeine)
- Works at method / service level.
- You choose what to cache (DTO, list, API response).
- More common in microservices for application-level caching.

Both can be used together in a real system.

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 Multi-level app caching (related interview angle)

Sometimes “L1 / L2” is also used for app architecture:

Request
  ↓
Local in-memory cache (Caffeine)   ← “L1”
  ↓
Distributed cache (Redis)          ← “L2”
  ↓
Database

If Redis fails → still use local cache / DB (graceful degradation).

Clarify with interviewer which meaning they want: Hibernate L1/L2 or app multi-level cache.

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Short Interview Answer

L1 cache is Hibernate’s first-level cache, enabled by default, and bound to a single Session/EntityManager.
Within one transaction, the same entity is loaded from DB only once and reused from memory.

L2 cache is the optional second-level cache at SessionFactory level, shared across sessions.
It needs a cache provider (Ehcache, etc.) and entity-level configuration.
Lookup order is L1 → L2 → Database.
L1 avoids duplicate queries in one unit of work; L2 reduces DB load across many requests.

